\begin_layout Section
Motivation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Great progress has been made in the field of Machine Learning (ML) in recent
 years, which means that more and more tasks previously reserved for humans
 can now be performed by machines.
 Reinforcement learning (RL), in which a machine learns independently to
 solve a problem without any previous knowledge, is of particular interest
 to industry.
 Tasks that were previously reserved for humans are thus not only solvable,
 the solution often even exceeds the known approaches.
 Examples are AlphaZero or AlphaGo, which achieved superhuman performance
 in the games of Chess and Go and could even beat all known chess computers.
  From observations of their way of playing, tactics could even be derived
 that have changed the way they play in the long term.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are also many areas in industry, such as controllers, which promise
 a lot of room for optimization.
 Nevertheless, there are great reservations about the use of such an approach
 because decisions cannot be explained and the process must therefore be
 considered a black box.
 This is not a problem for games or simulations, but in sensitive areas
 such as controlling a gas turbine or even a nuclear power plant, for example,
 this safety is essential.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The search for approaches in which the performance gain is not at the expense
 of lost control is therefore very great.
\end_layout

